    question: 'What is the difference between a JSA, a JHA and a toolbox talk?',
    answer:
      'JSA (job safety analysis) and JHA (job hazard analysis) are the same document under two names; some GCs and insurers prefer one term. A toolbox talk is a short crew safety meeting on a topic — ladders, heat, housekeeping — with a sign-in sheet, and is not tied to one task. On a high-risk day you do both: the toolbox talk covers the theme, the JSA covers the specific steps.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Does OSHA require a JSA?',
    answer:
      'OSHA’s construction standards require employers to identify hazards, provide the protection and training the hazards call for, and in some cases (fall protection plans, confined space, crane lifts) keep specific written plans — but they do not prescribe a form called a JSA. What makes the JSA effectively mandatory is the GC’s site-specific safety plan and your insurer: most require one for high-hazard tasks and ask for it after any incident. This is general information, not legal advice.',
  },
  {
    question: 'How many steps should a JSA have?',
    answer:
      'Six to ten. Fewer and the hazards hide inside steps that are too broad (“set the unit” covers rigging, lifting, landing and securing, each with different hazards). More than about twelve and the task is really two tasks — split it. The template has seven rows on the printed page and ten on the spreadsheet.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Who should fill out the JSA?',
    answer:
      'The person who knows the work and will be there — usually the foreman or the competent person for the task — with the crew’s input, then reviewed by a supervisor or the GC’s safety representative. A JSA written in the office by someone who will not be on the roof misses the hazard that matters, and a crew that did not help write it will not read it.',
  },
  {
    question: 'When does a JSA need to be revised?',
    answer:
      'Whenever the task, the crew, the location or the conditions change: a different lift path, a new crew member, wind picking up, a permit expiring, an unexpected utility. Note the revision date and reason on the form and re-review it with the crew. A JSA from last month is a template, not a plan.',
  },
  {
    question: 'How long should I keep signed JSAs?',
    answer:
      'At least for the life of the project plus whatever your safety program, your insurer and your contract require; many contractors keep them for several years. The signed JSA with the daily report for that day is what you produce after an incident, an OSHA visit or a claim — it shows the hazards were identified, controlled and communicated before the work started.',
  },
];
